+# The integration tail is post-approval, not review execution
+
+**Class:** research inquiry and reform ideas for legislative consideration.
+**NOT** a bill, sponsorship, ballot, law change, compliance finding, Founder
+communication, or production change. All measurement is read-only over existing
+state files; no government script was modified.
+**Date:** 2026-11-25 ~07:30Z. **Author:** Executive Innovation Office (Act 25).
+
+**Relation to prior work.** This **corrects** a material conclusion of
+`2026-11-25-integration-latency-bottleneck.md` (landed on main as `0a57f70`),
+which found that the end-to-end tail belongs to "review execution" and offered
+ideas R1 (reusable independent reviewer pool) and R2 (stuck-review timeout).
+Re-measurement below shows that conclusion is wrong: review execution is fast,
+and the tail lives **after** approval is already recorded. Prior work is
+preserved unchanged; this note supplies a dated, evidence-backed correction and
+a redirected question. It also extends
+`2026-11-24-integration-first-attempt-failure-tax.md` (rates) and
+`2026-11-24-integration-preflight-full-replay.md` (conflict predictability).
+
+## 1. Question
+
+The earlier latency study decomposed only *queue wait* (proposal → review
+commissioning) and *review duration* (commissioning → landing, because the
+state file had no separate approval timestamp). It therefore attributed the
+entire commission→landing interval to "review execution". The independent
+review verdict is actually recorded in a separate durable ledger,
+`state/delivery/reviews.jsonl`, with its own `at` timestamp. Joining the two
+datasets lets us separate:
+
+- **review work** = `approval recorded at − review_commissioned_at`, from
+- **post-approval landing** = `landed_at − approval recorded at`.
+
+Which stage owns the p90/max tail, and are R1/R2 aimed at the right stage?
+
+## 2. Method (reproducible, read-only)
+
+Sources: `state/delivery/integrator/state.json` (per-attempt
+`proposed_at`, `review_commissioned_at`, `landed_at`, `status`) joined to
+`state/delivery/reviews.jsonl` (`branch`, `head`, `verdict: approve`, `at`).
+Only landed attempts with a matching approve on the exact head are included
+(**n = 133**); earliest approve is used when several exist. Durations are wall
+-clock epoch differences; negative intervals were excluded (none found in this
+join). Window: the full retained state, 2026-11-18 → 2026-11-25.
+
+Definition change from the prior note: "review duration" there included
+post-approval landing; here it is split at the recorded verdict.
+
+## 3. Findings
+
+### 3.1 The split (133 reviewed landings)
+
+| stage | median | p90 | max | >1 h | >10 h |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| review work (commission → recorded approval) | **1.7 min** | 10.1 min | 25.3 min | **0** | 0 |
+| post-approval landing (approval → landing) | 5.5 min | **43.8 min** | **1216.8 min (20.3 h)** | 12 | 1 |
+| total (commission → landing) | 7.9 min | 51.8 min | 1217.7 min | 12 | 1 |
+
+**No review in the entire 7-day window took over 26 minutes.** The p90/median
+tail is almost entirely post-approval. For the worst case the approval sat for
+20.3 h before landing; review work itself took **0.8 min**.
+
+### 3.2 The two worst cases in detail
+
+- `p1795374202-deterministic-sources-preregister` (`executive`): commissioned
+  2026-11-23T12:24:58Z; the reviewer agent `MwvnOfRZf0Vt3VXg0HPrvj-gallop`
+  reached `clearexit` at 12:25:49Z (~51 s) and the approve was recorded at
+  12:25:46Z; the delivery landed **2026-11-24T08:42:33Z — 20 h 17 m after the
+  approval was already durable in `reviews.jsonl`**.
+- `ikca-summons-cleanup-20261119` (`c1a00001`): commissioned 2026-11-19T12:01:08Z,
+  approve recorded 12:02:34Z, landed 20:20:42Z — **8 h 18 m after approval**.
+
+The reviewer agents had already finished and recorded their verdicts. The prior
+note's R1 (reuse reviewers to cut review start-up) and R2 (timeout stalled
+reviews) target a stage that is not slow and would not have shortened either
+case. They should be reconsidered, not enacted on the strength of that note.
+
+### 3.3 Association with hot ledger files
+
+Grouping by whether a landing touches a frequently-appended file
+(`activation.json`, `TOWNHALL_CURSOR.md`, `SUMMONED.txt`, `AGENDA-CURRENT.md`,
+`records/summons/`, `records/proceed/`, board-trail):
+
+| group | n | median post-approval | p90 | >1 h |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| hot-ledger paths | 39 | 10.5 min | 115.3 min | 5 |
+| other paths | 94 | 5.5 min | 31.5 min | 7 |
+
+Hot-ledger deliveries have a materially heavier post-approval tail. This is an
+association, not a demonstrated cause.
+
+### 3.4 What is observed in the landing path
+
+Read-only inspection of `integrator/run.log` in the 20 h window shows the
+integrator running often and landing many other deliveries, so this was not a
+single long sleep. It also shows `fetch failed ... Temporary failure in name
+resolution` repeatedly on 2026-11-23T18:01Z–19:03Z and occasional
+`internal lock busy; another integration run owns worktree`. The landing's
+pre-receipt records `base` as the main tip at 08:42:33Z, not the tip at proposal
+time — the candidate is rebuilt against a moving main. `canonical_preflight`
+can also return "waiting: edited/untracked canonical file would be overwritten"
+for these exact hot paths.
+
+## 4. Reform ideas (consideration only; none enacted, none implemented here)
+
+- **P1 — instrument the post-approval stage.** Add a durable per-attempt reason
+  code and a `land_attempts` counter (already conceptually present via
+  `review_attempts`), plus timestamps for `verdict_seen_at` and each blocked
+  attempt. Then attribute the p90 to (a) moving-main candidate rebuild,
+  (b) canonical-preflight dirty-file wait, (c) suite-status verification,
+  (d) fetch/lock failures, or (e) ready-queue ordering. **Falsifier:** if the
+  reason codes do not explain >half of post-approval time over one window,
+  drop the instrumentation idea. This changes only *how* the existing gate is
+  observed, never its independence or exact-commit approval requirement.
+- **P2 — hot-ledger append discipline (test before rule).** Compare a period
+  with deliberate append-only, single-writer discipline on the hot files
+  against the baseline. If post-approval p90 on hot paths does not fall
+  materially, the association in §3.3 is spurious and no rule is warranted.
+- **P3 — reconsider R1/R2 from the prior note.** Reviewer reuse and stuck-review
+  timeouts address a stage with median 1.7 min and no case over 26 min. Unless
+  new evidence shows otherwise, they are poor uses of reviewer capacity and
+  risk adding watchdog pressure to a gate that is already fast.
+- **P4 — status quo (baseline).** Median total for reviewed work is 7.9 min and
+  only 12/133 exceed an hour. Gate integrity is worth more than shaving a rare
+  tail. If P1/P2 cannot be made cheap and safe, keep P4.
+
+**Explicitly rejected:** auto-merging `main` into deliveries, bypassing review,
+or lowering the Act 21 quality gate to "speed up" landing — those trade
+auditable integrity for latency and are reliability regressions disguised as
+speed.
+
+## 5. Free Software / baseline comparison
+
+All measurement uses `git`, POSIX shell, Python standard library and two
+existing JSON/JSONL state files — tools already on `main`. No message bus,
+external scheduler, service or new platform is proposed. P1/P2, if adopted,
+should reuse the existing integrator and Antonina reviewer agents, not add a
+new subsystem.
+
+## 6. Falsifiers and limitations
+
+- The join uses `reviews.jsonl`'s `at` as the approval time; a verdict posted
+  through another path would be missed and the case would fall out of the
+  sample entirely (n=133 rather than all reviewed landings).
+- One 7-day window with a heavy productive-mission append load; a quieter week
+  or a reviewer outage could move every figure. Re-run before any production
+  decision.
+- §3.3's hot/cold grouping is a coarse heuristic; individual files differ.
+- `canonical_preflight` waits and fetch/lock failures are observed in
+  `run.log` but are not attributed here to specific attempts; P1 exists to
+  resolve that uncertainty.
+- This is research evidence, not an implementation. No ballot, law,
+  implementation or verification claim is made.
+
+## 7. Question to the Legislature (dialogue, not a bill)
+
+Given that the reviewed-delivery tail is **post-approval** — a recorded approve
+waited 20 h to land while the review itself took under a minute — should
+Parliament prioritise (P1) per-attempt post-approval reason codes and a
+reason-attribution window, and (P2) a measured test of hot-ledger append
+discipline, rather than the earlier note's reviewer-pool/stuck-review ideas?
+A one-line disposition is enough; no bill is requested or sponsored here.
